\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1d25d6-8ed3-59df-911c-7d0cb3fdc8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469044} {:answer/id #uuid "5ceb7842-8979-5416-aad4-760e628285f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ceb7842-8979-5416-aad4-760e628285f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469045} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42f00c6-6c84-5fb6-8df5-83a524e29e2c" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21598207-e841-5f39-8f2a-4ec2cfbec0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469046} {:answer/id #uuid "c901c73f-ddc2-5d19-aeaa-5345c49b20f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21598207-e841-5f39-8f2a-4ec2cfbec0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469046} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45cb722-742a-5576-a0d9-c95cfa6bdb62" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ba564d-eeea-5879-b8d0-f329022e980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469048} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4543b5-7c0b-5482-af32-ce6dac305545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74ba564d-eeea-5879-b8d0-f329022e980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469048} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7852e2-0fb8-517d-a85f-e3a3e578317a" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c1b6231-ddc6-5ac3-a5c8-9d11b32158e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47456" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490508} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491234}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffbbdf5b-b5a9-58a5-b789-13db6ff1a8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469014} {:answer/id #uuid "725a5695-1253-5d33-8d18-e5f71e632c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffbbdf5b-b5a9-58a5-b789-13db6ff1a8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469014} :answer-field/id #uuid "134c0d8a-c3e3-5d31-a016-da1dea915d49" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c68912f2-a32e-5af0-bfd8-53a4e352fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469016} {:answer/id #uuid "90511e22-e82e-54b5-a8be-46168f1b6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90511e22-e82e-54b5-a8be-46168f1b6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469017} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d16e520-7b60-5e2f-8e42-9334e0996e9c" :answer-field/key "If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e45344d-c214-5cf1-9c6c-7c83274e9ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469018} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea614b-40b5-59b4-ac19-8dc8a355b72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e45344d-c214-5cf1-9c6c-7c83274e9ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469018} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f93777f-8292-5756-b7f9-4905592e30dc" :answer-field/key "If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "006b1b75-818e-5ba9-8f81-bc35ede44097" :question/math-academy-id "q-38141" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4e29d4-8ab4-51b2-bed7-3d933724288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469038} {:answer/id #uuid "366b643d-0477-56b8-99e8-05665c95ecc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e29d4-8ab4-51b2-bed7-3d933724288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469038} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa806480-98e3-5de9-ac37-45bb7e60daee" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dba33dd-efd6-5edd-8cb4-431f80150830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469040} {:answer/id #uuid "03dbdbf2-dd97-5434-bd34-a6b20449669a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dba33dd-efd6-5edd-8cb4-431f80150830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469040} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad0d392-5687-58fc-8043-83455bbb6881" :answer-field/key "If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470132} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5c5fde-9d69-5dd0-bd3e-c686ea921e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469042} {:answer/id #uuid "c12380e7-7519-5b6e-8745-f0b879e1df9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5c5fde-9d69-5dd0-bd3e-c686ea921e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469042} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9fc1cb-34d2-5ac7-b925-bd6d7335fddb" :answer-field/key "If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "936f721f-93be-5c51-8233-7da49b500101" :question/math-academy-id "q-38979" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6b563a-739c-5aa6-aa3d-91cf2bda7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469302} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae5e117-a505-5a04-81e4-439b74052c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ae5e117-a505-5a04-81e4-439b74052c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469303} :answer-field/id #uuid "0829ea20-3a9e-5bac-9aac-a20a5031708f" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ea0788-dc9f-5b43-b4d9-3e6d92f4a3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469304} {:answer/id #uuid "16e976b2-1f3b-5daf-bc6f-ee9f3ff92e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e976b2-1f3b-5daf-bc6f-ee9f3ff92e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469305} :answer-field/id #uuid "55766021-6ec3-5fd5-927e-a3d5966052e8" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584ceae8-0e69-5969-b025-a8dfde1e4426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469306} {:answer/id #uuid "24a75a78-d9f9-590b-98ed-a75a55f1195e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584ceae8-0e69-5969-b025-a8dfde1e4426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469306} :answer-field/id #uuid "44fe36b7-f90a-541a-a159-56cfdbf08c21" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f6a64ec-417d-5f3c-a079-819cbc656ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83440" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{3}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$; $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505097} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505856}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f9232e-9658-51ed-95f2-ac2fd1e78bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454078} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49195-0754-59e8-a736-789d713df706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49195-0754-59e8-a736-789d713df706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454079} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fee3505-d7fb-511a-a74e-2a2d44598ed9" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b58ec5-18ed-565a-8a4c-7a428ef3b660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454080} {:answer/id #uuid "fa80fb0a-3883-56b5-b6eb-070e8e31d145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33b58ec5-18ed-565a-8a4c-7a428ef3b660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454080} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d6a4961-38f7-5264-b58e-a39849428f01" :answer-field/key "If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642014a7-57ea-5677-bbda-439505c688fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454082} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeac619-768a-5b27-a0a3-6f578395c470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642014a7-57ea-5677-bbda-439505c688fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f9063c-3f2e-5e1f-893e-6358b44768be" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84081" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$” is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible” is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406809} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406844}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec4bc02-0df9-5348-9ee4-473b64cacd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472718} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc239d3-5a4c-5fa5-98b1-768823c16b23" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd1213a-a7a4-5890-88d8-8de888db8343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff1d1ba-36f4-5b99-8a47-88e5119e65f7" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} {:answer/id #uuid "01638de6-6ede-5e18-812c-b75ad72b29e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec578f4-3243-5145-b140-1c5ad943604d" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140100" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406809} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406846}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a8fd7b-d587-5f84-abef-00aeb093eb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c74f36-a3a0-589c-a45b-af2f83dc7942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a8fd7b-d587-5f84-abef-00aeb093eb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} :answer-field/id #uuid "88425be4-23e4-530d-894d-7ba2ee67cacc" :answer-field/key "$T$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b1c9af-14b9-5566-a390-e0cfcfb93713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453894} {:answer/id #uuid "56beb87c-fb76-5782-843d-1bb2ef34ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56beb87c-fb76-5782-843d-1bb2ef34ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eccd02e-cd48-5da5-a4d4-40a643e437ff" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cc4c4f-6b01-5029-937b-d1096d770d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} {:answer/id #uuid "998a225c-9aad-55b1-9522-e1b176e1886c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cc4c4f-6b01-5029-937b-d1096d770d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b61e149-64e1-57db-98c8-d0a11bc647eb" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079" :question/math-academy-id "q-38893" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dae18-53b8-50d8-9953-c286f053f130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473452} {:answe